V EXIT CODES: 4 declared verdict code(s) that `gates/gatekit.py` has no name for, and 10
   declared code(s) the owner HAS a name for, under a different one. A gate declaring an unowned one is saying `exit [0, 1, 3, 4, 5]`
   means something here that the tree does not know, and an aggregator either guesses or calls it DEAD;
   a gate declaring a RENAMED one is worse in the way that matters least and most: the code is
   known, so a runner charging by integer silently prints the OWNER'S word for a gate that never used it.
   Both are `refusalsweep`'s failure: a gate that crashes where it should have refused cannot say which.

